    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166814,
      "id": "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f",
      "title": "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1b739ad6-f82d-5ee4-af81-14bfbaf6dc18",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=e^{y}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x^{3}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=e^{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "973495cd-5c46-5f4f-8e45-6034d982b733",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
